> lab-mail one service at a time, and patch 6 sets up lab-claude as the
> client.
>
> Testing. None of this has run. lab-claude has no doas and none of
> smtprelay, Dovecot, mbsync, or msmtp installed, so I could not build
> the jail, start the services, or run the acceptance test. What I did
> check:
> - I read the sources the design depends on: smtprelay 1.14.0 (the
>   version in ports), its SMTP server library chrj/smtpd 1.0.0, msmtp
>   1.8.34, and isync 1.5.1. The findings below come from that reading.
> - I tested the allowlist patterns with grep -iE. They accept
>   pat@patmaddox.com, x@p7x.dev, and lab@lists.p7x.dev in any case.
>   They reject someone@example.com, x@sub.p7x.dev, x@p7x.devx,
>   x@lists.p7x.dev.evil.com, and someone@p7x.dev as a sender. RE2 and
>   ERE agree on patterns this simple, but that is inference; smtprelay
>   itself did not run.
> - I ran the fetch script's chmod step on a scratch maildir with 0700
>   directories and 0600 files. It left them at 0755 and 0644.
> - I ran the notmuch profile config with notmuch 0.40. It indexes a
>   test maildir and skips mbsync's state files.
> - I listed both dist trees through tar and dist.mtree.
>
> The Dovecot config is the least certain part, since I could not run
> doveconf on it. Running "doveconf -n" in the jail is the first check
> I would make.
>
> To try it on beastie, store the secrets as lab-mail's README.org
> describes, then run "just build install start" in lab-mail, and stop
> and start it once so the services pick up their config. Then rebuild
> lab-claude and run the acceptance test from the spec.
>
> Where the implementation and the spec disagree. Each of these needs
> your call, and I can send a spec patch for any you accept.
>
> 1. smtprelay refuses a sender or recipient with 451, not 5xx. The
>    code is hard-coded in senderChecker and recipientChecker in
>    main.go; there is no setting. msmtp still exits non-zero (69,
>    EX_UNAVAILABLE) and the message goes to no one, so acceptance steps
>    5 and 6 hold. Only the "5xx" wording fails. A 4xx tells a queuing
>    client to retry, but no client here queues. I suggest amending the
>    spec to say 4xx, and offering smtprelay upstream a patch to answer
>    550.
>
> 2. msmtp does send DATA. smtpd advertises PIPELINING, so msmtp sends
>    MAIL, every RCPT, and DATA in one batch. When a RCPT is refused,
>    msmtp closes the connection without sending the message.
>    smtpd sees the connection end before the closing dot, treats it as
>    a network error, and delivers nothing (handleDATA in protocol.go).
>    So the outcome the spec wants holds, but its sentence "msmtp sends
>    no DATA" is wrong. "msmtp sends no message" would be right.
>
> 3. smtprelay adds a Received header to each message before relaying
>    it. The spec says it does not rewrite the headers. Every MTA adds
>    one, and RFC 5321 requires it. I suggest the spec say it adds only
>    a Received line.
>
> 4. Dovecot serves an empty INBOX next to lists. IMAP reserves INBOX
>    as every user's primary mailbox, and Dovecot requires an inbox
>    namespace. The INBOX lives in vmail's own directory and the ACL
>    makes it read-only, so nothing can ever land in it.
>
> 5. mbsync creates mail 0600 and folders 0700, hard-coded in
>    drv_maildir.c, whatever the umask. So vmail could not read the
>    maildir at all. The fetch job opens new files for reading after
>    each pull. Until it does, a message that just arrived cannot be
>    read over IMAP. A client that tries in that moment should get an
>    error and picks the message up on its next run. The alternatives
>    were running Dovecot as mbsync, which breaks "Dovecot's user
>    cannot write the maildir", or inherited ZFS ACLs, which depend on
>    the dataset's aclinherit and aclmode settings that I cannot see
>    from here.
>
> 6. The secrets directory holds the Fastmail login as well as the app
>    password, in two files, user and password. Both smtprelay and
>    mbsync need the login, and the spec does not say where it lives.
>
> 7. The spec calls the Fastmail folder "lists/". mbsyncrc uses the
>    IMAP name "lists". If Fastmail shows it under another name, such
>    as INBOX/lists, only the Far line in lab-mail's mbsyncrc changes.
>
> One more note on patch 6. The lists notmuch profile names its
> database path, though notmuch would derive the same path from
> NOTMUCH_PROFILE. When I tested the config through NOTMUCH_CONFIG,
> with no path set, notmuch indexed the test maildir into the default
> profile's database and dropped the list from it until I ran notmuch
> new again. With the path set, that cannot happen.
